  data: {
    LLM: "In a game of pure chance, there is no mathematical way to 'Win' Petit Paquet, but there is a way to 'Manage Losses'. Spread your bets! Never put all your tokens on a single packet. If you spread across three packets, you only need one card to be higher than the Banker's to potentially break even (depending on house rules for ties).\n\nThe 'Banker's Fatigue': Watch the deck. If many Aces and Kings have already been revealed in the first few rounds of a deck, the Banker's pile is statistically more likely to turn up lower cards in the late-game. This is when you should increase your bets on the Punters' side.\n\nBanker Management: The Banker has a massive statistical advantage because they win on all 'Ties'. If you have the opportunity to serve as Banker, take it! However, you must have enough capital (the 'Bankroll') to pay out simultaneously if all 10 packets reveal higher cards than yours. A Banker with a small bankroll is easily 'Broken'.",
    Player: "In a game of pure chance, there is no mathematical way to 'Win' Petit Paquet, but there is a way to 'Manage Losses'. Spread your bets! Never put all your tokens on a single packet. If you spread across three packets, you only need one card to be higher than the Banker's to potentially break even (depending on house rules for ties).\n\nThe 'Banker's Fatigue': Watch the deck. If many Aces and Kings have already been revealed in the first few rounds of a deck, the Banker's pile is statistically more likely to turn up lower cards in the late-game. This is when you should increase your bets on the Punters' side.\n\nBanker Management: The Banker has a massive statistical advantage because they win on all 'Ties'. If you have the opportunity to serve as Banker, take it! However, you must have enough capital (the 'Bankroll') to pay out simultaneously if all 10 packets reveal higher cards than yours. A Banker with a small bankroll is easily 'Broken'.",
